**Clarify.** API: `bool allow(clientId, timestamp)`. Limit: at most `N` requests in any sliding window of `W` seconds. Per-client state. Assume timestamps are non-decreasing; mention using a monotonic clock in production.

**Algorithm (sliding window log with a queue).** For each client keep a queue of timestamps of accepted requests.

1. On each request at time `t`, **evict** from the front of the queue every timestamp `<= t - W` (they have left the window).
2. If `queue.size() < N`, push `t` and return **true**.
3. Otherwise return **false** (rejected requests are not recorded).

Each timestamp is pushed once and popped once, so the amortized time is **O(1) per request**; memory is **O(N)** per client.

    class RateLimiter {
        int limit; long window;
        unordered_map<string, deque<long>> logs;
        mutex mu;
    public:
        RateLimiter(int n, long w) : limit(n), window(w) {}
        bool allow(const string& id, long t) {
            lock_guard<mutex> g(mu);
            auto& q = logs[id];
            while (!q.empty() && q.front() <= t - window) q.pop_front();
            if ((int)q.size() < limit) { q.push_back(t); return true; }
            return false;
        }
    };

**Edge cases.** Window boundary: decide whether the window is `(t-W, t]`; empty queue; first request; multiple requests with the same timestamp; clock going backwards (use a monotonic clock).

**Concurrency.** A single mutex is simple but contends; use per-client locks or shard the map by hash of the client id. Check-and-insert must happen under the same lock.

**Trade-offs and alternatives.** The log is exact but uses O(N) memory per client; a **fixed window counter** is cheaper but allows boundary bursts; a **sliding window counter** approximates with two counters; a **token bucket** allows controlled bursts with O(1) memory. For many servers, keep state in Redis with atomic operations.

**Housekeeping.** Remove idle clients' entries to bound memory. Say you would write tests for exact limit, window expiry and rejection not consuming quota.
